{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 39, "model_name": "meta/llama-3.1-8b-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a member of the House of Poitiers and the ruler of the County of Tripoli. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Born around 1152 he was the heir to the County of Tripoli which was a Crusader state in the Levant.\n\n* **Family and Early Life** *\nRaymond III was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. His mother was the daughter of Melisende of Jerusalem and Fulk of Anjou. He had a sister named Melisende Countess of Tripoli. Raymond III's family was of noble origin and had strong ties to the Kingdom of Jerusalem.\n\nHe succeeded his father in 1152 and became the Count of Tripoli at the age of around 12. His early years as count were under the regency of his mother Hodierna of Jerusalem. In 1153 he married Eschiva of Bures. The couple had one son named Bohemond III of Tripoli who would succeed Raymond III as the Count of Tripoli.\n\n* **Reign and Conflicts** *\nRaymond III faced several conflicts during his reign. In 1164 he joined the Second Crusade led by King Amalric I of Jerusalem. The crusade was aimed at defeating the Ayyubid dynasty which was expanding its territory in the Levant. However the crusade failed to achieve its objectives and Raymond III returned to Tripoli.\n\nIn 1170 Raymond III married Stephanie of Milly. The couple had a daughter named Melisende of Tripoli. He also had a second daughter named Alice of Tripoli. In 1173 he supported the rebellion of his brother-in-law Baldwin IV of Jerusalem against the regency of his mother Sibylla of Jerusalem. However the rebellion failed and Raymond III was forced to flee to the County of Tripoli.\n\n* **Later Life and Death** *\nIn 1187 Raymond III joined the Third Crusade led by King Richard the Lionheart of England. He was present at the Battle of Arsuf in 1191 where the crusaders defeated the Ayyubid